<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercisegroup xml:id="exercises-power-sets" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Power sets
  </title>
  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Write down the power sets of the given sets in roster notation.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Write down the power set <m> \ps \of{ S } </m> of the set <m> S = \set{ 1 } </m> in roster notation.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <answer>
      <p>
        The power set <m> \ps \of{ S } </m> of the set <m> S = \set{ 1 } </m> is the set
        
        <md>
          \ps \of{ S } = \set{ \emptyset , \set{ 1 } }
        </md>.
      </p>
    </answer>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        Choosing a subset of the set <m> S = \set{ 1 } </m> is equivalent to making a unique binary choices of whether or not to include the element <m> 1 </m>. We see that <m> S </m> has the following <m> 2 </m> subsets: <m> \emptyset </m>, and <m> \set{ 1 } </m>. Therefore, the power set <m> \ps \of{ S } </m> of the set <m> S = \set{ 1 } </m> is the set
        
        <md>
          \ps \of{ S } = \set{ \emptyset , \set{ 1 } }
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Write down the power set <m> \ps \of{ T } </m> of the set <m> T = \set{ 1 , 2 } </m> in roster notation.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <answer>
      <p>
        The power set <m> \ps \of{ T } </m> of the set <m> T = \set{ 1 , 2 } </m> is the set
        
        <md>
          \ps \of{ T } = \set{ \emptyset , \set{ 1 } , \set{ 2 } , \set{ 1 , 2 } }
        </md>.
      </p>
    </answer>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        Choosing a subset of the set <m> T = \set{ 1 , 2 } </m> is equivalent to making a unique sequence of two binary choices of whether to include each element <m> 1 </m> and <m> 2 </m>. We see that <m> T </m> has the following <m> 4 </m> subsets: <m> \emptyset </m>, <m> \set{ 1 } </m>, <m> \set{ 2 } </m>, and <m> \set{ 1 , 2 } </m>. Therefore, the power set <m> \ps \of{ T } </m> of the set <m> S = \set{ 1 , 2 } </m> is the set
        
        <md>
          \ps \of{ T } = \set{ \emptyset , \set{ 1 } , \set{ 2 } , \set{ 1 , 2 } }
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Write down the power set <m> \ps \of{ U } </m> of the set <m> U = \set{ 1 , 2 , 3 } </m> in roster notation.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <answer>
      <p>
        The power set <m> \ps \of{ U } </m> of the set <m> U = \set{ 1 , 2 , 3 } </m> is the set
        
        <md>
          \ps \of{ U } = \set{ \emptyset , \set{ 1 } , \set{ 2 } , \set{ 3 } , \set{ 1 , 2 } , \set{ 1 , 3 } , \set{ 2 , 3 } , \set{ 1 , 2 , 3 } }
        </md>.
      </p>
    </answer>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        Choosing a subset of the set <m> U = \set{ 1 , 2 , 3 } </m> is equivalent to making a unique sequence of three binary choices of whether to include each element <m> 1 </m>, <m> 2 </m>, and <m> 3 </m>. We see that <m> U </m> has the following <m> 8 </m> subsets: <m> \emptyset </m>, <m> \set{ 1 } </m>, <m> \set{ 2 } </m>, <m> \set{ 3 } </m>, <m> \set{ 1 , 2 } </m>, <m> \set{ 1 , 3 } </m>, <m> \set{ 2 , 3 } </m>, and <m> \set{ 1 , 2 , 3 } </m>. Therefore, the power set <m> \ps \of{ U } </m> of the set <m> U = \set{ 1 , 2 , 3 } </m> is the set
        
        <md>
          \ps \of{ U } = \set{ \emptyset , \set{ 1 } , \set{ 2 } , \set{ 3 } , \set{ 1 , 2 } , \set{ 1 , 3 } , \set{ 2 , 3 } , \set{ 1 , 2 , 3 } }
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
</exercisegroup>
